WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.395
Hi, I'm Tapioca!

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:04.605
Everyone quotes twenty tokens per parameter.

00:00:04.605 --> 00:00:07.920
Models shipping now run thousands of times past it.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.762
Here's why both are right.

00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:13.356
Loss falls predictably as you spend.

00:00:13.356 --> 00:00:17.283
But the best way to split the spend depends on which costs

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.574
you count: training, serving, or inference samples.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.308
Kaplan and colleagues found loss falls as a power law in

00:00:24.308 --> 00:00:25.548
parameters, data and compute.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:27.688
It doesn't describe the walk downhill.

00:00:27.688 --> 00:00:30.554
It predicts how low the floor sits once training ends.

00:00:30.974 --> 00:00:35.724
On log axes it's a straight line, across more than seven orders of magnitude.

00:00:35.724 --> 00:00:38.099
That makes big runs a planning exercise.

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:40.205
But the exponent is tiny.

00:00:40.205 --> 00:00:44.588
Ten times the compute removes only about eleven percent of the remaining loss.

00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:47.011
The line held.

00:00:47.011 --> 00:00:49.470
What changed is which costs go into the budget.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.101
Kaplan's reading: for a training budget, spend on parameters.

00:00:53.521 --> 00:00:57.910
Chinchilla trained over four hundred models and found both should grow together:

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.739
about twenty tokens per parameter.

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:01.868
But serving is paid per parameter.

00:01:01.868 --> 00:01:05.002
Put it in the objective, and the optimum moves to a

00:01:05.002 --> 00:01:06.141
hundred to two hundred.

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:10.471
Add the samples drawn at inference, and it shifts further, well into

00:01:10.471 --> 00:01:11.774
over-training a smaller model.

00:01:12.925 --> 00:01:17.477
So models shipping now run at two hundred and fourteen tokens per

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.133
parameter, at sixty thousand, even seventy-nine thousand.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.271
That isn't drift.

00:01:21.271 --> 00:01:22.788
It's a different objective.

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.155
A scaling law is a conditional statement: before quoting a ratio, ask

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:29.879
which budget it was optimising.

00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:36.103
To recap: a straight but shallow line, twenty to one for training

00:01:36.103 --> 00:01:38.850
alone, and over-training once serving and sampling count.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.544
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.544 --> 00:01:42.194
I'm Tapioca.

00:01:42.194 --> 00:01:42.519
Bye!
